WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:03.890
The death of Iron Man in Avengers: Endgame
was as heartbreaking as a superhero death

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:07.970
can be, and actor Robert Downey Jr. was the
mastermind behind one of the most wrenching

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.640
aspects of Tony Stark's passing.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.840
As fans know, Tony Stark sacrificed himself
during the final battle against Thanos at

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.000
the end of Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.720
After retrieving the Infinity Stones just
as everyone thought the Mad Titan had them

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.980
in his possession, Tony looked Thanos square
in his big, dumb, purple face, declared…

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.200
"I… am…

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:31.760
Iron Man."

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:32.960
…and snapped his fingers.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.790
Thanos, his army of Chitauri, his Black Order
sycophants, and his fleet of spaceships all

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.820
crumbled away to space dust, marking a big
win for Earth's Mightiest Heroes.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.000
It came at a cost, of course, in the form
of Tony Stark's life.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.649
Slipping away due to the damage he suffered
from using the Infinity Stones, all Tony could

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:53.780
manage was a faint "Hey, Pep" when Pepper
Potts came to say goodbye.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.980
Devastating for sure, but not quite the enduring
final words we'd expect from Iron Man.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.240
So, why didn't Iron Man have any official
dying words in Avengers: Endgame?

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.670
According to screenwriters Christopher Markus
and Stephen McFeely, it was Downey Jr.'s idea

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:09.800
to not complicate Iron Man's death with any
final lines after "I am Iron Man," in order

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.620
to make the moment that much more impactful.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.380
They explained in during their San Diego Comic-Con
2019 panel that a previous draft of the Endgame

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.090
script saw Tony chattering away - as was typical
for the cocky hero - but Downey Jr. didn't

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.090
think it felt right.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.040
McFeely explained:

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.840
"These actors have spent a lot of time with
these characters.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.979
So Chris and I are very happy [to], and did,
write all sorts of lovely dying words for

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:30.600
Tony Stark.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.400
Robert is not happy to say them, right?

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.920
Robert wants to - and knew this instinctively
- [that Tony is] a guy who has talked and

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.960
talked and talked for many, many movies.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.680
When he doesn't talk, you are crushed.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.560
And he knew that, and we didn't feel we could
turn in a page where he didn't talk."

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.290
Ultimately, Downey Jr. told McFeely and Markus
that he wanted to, quote, "do much, much less"

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.360
with Iron Man's death, which McFeely admitted
was the absolute right call in the end.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.520
Tony Stark's passing in Avengers: Endgame
went through multiple rewrites, one of which

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.260
was drastically different than even the version
where the hero was talkative as he's slouched

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.740
over and knocking on death's door.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.820
One draft of the script that actually made
it to shooting saw Thanos spit at Iron Man,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.569
"I am inevitable," and the Armored Avenger
snap his fingers without saying a word.

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:13.650
Endgame directors Joe and Anthony Russo shot
that version of the scene, which was in tact

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.109
all the way until the editing phase of production,
before editor Jeff Ford suggested that Tony

00:02:18.109 --> 00:02:19.080
say, "I am Iron Man."

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.360
Downey Jr. was originally opposed to delivering
any kind of line - even one that calls back

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.069
to the iconic confession scene in 2008's Iron
Man when Tony Stark reveals his superhero

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:28.560
identity…

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:33.620
"I am Iron Man."

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.440
…and it took some convincing for the actor
to come back for reshoots.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.459
Thankfully, Endgame producer Joel Silver,
who is an old friend of Downey Jr.'s, was

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:48.230
present at the dinner where the Russos pitched
the new idea and was able to get Downey Jr.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:48.720
on board.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.720
Tony Stark may have been a hero who "lived
and died by quips," as Avengers: Endgame director

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.860
Joe Russo once said, but it's hard to imagine
his death going any other way than it did.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.760
The scene wound up striking the perfect balance
between a powerful full-circle moment with

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.569
the "I am Iron Man" line, and a minimalistic
approach that offered a crushing contrast

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:05.840
to Tony's chatty nature.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.880
And it's largely thanks to Downey Jr.

